// What two panels facing each other are left with when together they ask for
// more than there is.
//
// The third and last part of the panel decision.  "strut-span" answers
// whether a claim touches this monitor; "strut-reserve" answers by how much
// the monitor shrinks because of it.  Both look at ONE edge and know nothing
// about the other.  So both let the overrun through: a panel 28 deep on top
// is modest, a panel 28 deep on the bottom is modest, and on a region 40 tall
// the two of them together are impossible.
//
// This is the fact about the PAIR, and it is written down as a model rather
// than as a line in screen.c for exactly one reason: it is a number, and
// until now it was the only ribbon arithmetic without a model
// (doc/portability.md, "what to do next").
//
// The near edge wins the tie.  That is a choice, not a law: someone has to
// give, and giving at the far edge keeps the top of the region where a reader
// expects it and pushes the loss to the bottom - where the status bar lives
// and where the loss is NOTICED rather than guessed at.
//
// Both depths arrive from "strut-reserve" and are therefore already
// non-negative and each no larger than the region.  The model does not lean
// on that - it repairs negatives too - but the reasoning about it does.
//
// The caller computes the differences and passes them as fields, because an
// FTS condition compares one field with a constant or with a percent of one
// field:
//
//   overrun     near strut + far strut - region length
//   far-left    region length - near strut
//
// The measured case to check against: a region 800 pixels tall, a panel 28 on
// top and 28 on the bottom - no overrun, each gets what it asked for, the
// columns become 744.  The argument starts where the region is small: 40
// pixels for two panels of 28 gives 28 at the top and 12 at the bottom.

category "Ribbon layout"

  object "Facing pair"
    near-strut is number
    far-strut is number
    region-length is number
    want-far is boolean
    overrun is number
    far-left is number

  utility strut-pair
    accepts "Facing pair"
    returns number
    starts with 0

    // The order decides everything here: every rule whose condition holds
    // runs, and the last "then result equals" overwrites the ones before it.
    // First "they fit", then the argument - exactly the way the C reads top
    // to bottom.

    rule "They fit: the near edge gets all it asked for"
      if overrun is at most 0
      and want-far equals false
      then result equals 100 percent of field near-strut

    rule "They fit: the far edge gets all it asked for"
      if overrun is at most 0
      and want-far equals true
      then result equals 100 percent of field far-strut

    rule "Argument: the near edge keeps its own in full"
      if overrun is greater than 0
      and want-far equals false
      then result equals 100 percent of field near-strut

    rule "Argument: the near edge is deeper than the region and takes it whole"
      if overrun is greater than 0
      and want-far equals false
      and near-strut is greater than 100 percent of field region-length
      then result equals 100 percent of field region-length

    rule "Argument: the far edge gets what the near one did not take"
      if overrun is greater than 0
      and want-far equals true
      then result equals 100 percent of field far-left

    rule "Argument: the near edge took everything and the far one gets none"
      if overrun is greater than 0
      and want-far equals true
      and far-left is less than 0
      then result equals 0

    rule "There is no such thing as a negative near strut"
      if near-strut is less than 0
      and want-far equals false
      then result equals 0

    rule "There is no such thing as a negative far strut"
      if far-strut is less than 0
      and want-far equals true
      then result equals 0

    property "Nothing less than nothing is given up"
      result is at least 0

    property "Nothing more than the whole region is given up"
      result is at most 100 percent of field region-length

    example "Two panels of 28 on a screen of 800: no overrun, the near one keeps its own"
      given near-strut equals 28
      given far-strut equals 28
      given region-length equals 800
      given want-far equals false
      given overrun equals -744
      given far-left equals 772
      expected result equals 28

    example "Two panels of 28 on a screen of 800: no overrun, the far one keeps its own"
      given near-strut equals 28
      given far-strut equals 28
      given region-length equals 800
      given want-far equals true
      given overrun equals -744
      given far-left equals 772
      expected result equals 28

    example "A region of 40 for two panels of 28: the near one gets all 28"
      given near-strut equals 28
      given far-strut equals 28
      given region-length equals 40
      given want-far equals false
      given overrun equals 16
      given far-left equals 12
      expected result equals 28

    example "A region of 40 for two panels of 28: the far one gets the remaining 12"
      given near-strut equals 28
      given far-strut equals 28
      given region-length equals 40
      given want-far equals true
      given overrun equals 16
      given far-left equals 12
      expected result equals 12

    example "The near edge is deeper than the whole region: it takes the region"
      given near-strut equals 50
      given far-strut equals 10
      given region-length equals 40
      given want-far equals false
      given overrun equals 20
      given far-left equals -10
      expected result equals 40

    example "The near edge is deeper than the whole region: the far one gets zero"
      given near-strut equals 50
      given far-strut equals 10
      given region-length equals 40
      given want-far equals true
      given overrun equals 20
      given far-left equals -10
      expected result equals 0

    example "Together they fill the region exactly: no argument"
      given near-strut equals 20
      given far-strut equals 20
      given region-length equals 40
      given want-far equals true
      given overrun equals 0
      given far-left equals 20
      expected result equals 20

    example "Together they are one pixel over: the far edge pays that pixel"
      given near-strut equals 20
      given far-strut equals 21
      given region-length equals 40
      given want-far equals true
      given overrun equals 1
      given far-left equals 20
      expected result equals 20

    example "No panels at all"
      given near-strut equals 0
      given far-strut equals 0
      given region-length equals 800
      given want-far equals false
      given overrun equals -800
      given far-left equals 800
      expected result equals 0

    example "A region of zero length gives up nothing"
      given near-strut equals 28
      given far-strut equals 28
      given region-length equals 0
      given want-far equals false
      given overrun equals 56
      given far-left equals -28
      expected result equals 0
